WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.240
Okay.

00:04.240 --> 00:07.360
So now we're going to talk about the error handling in stored procedure.

00:07.720 --> 00:08.440
Error handling.

00:08.440 --> 00:14.440
It is like essential things to do while programming because it gives you the control on what can happen

00:14.440 --> 00:15.600
once you have an error.

00:15.600 --> 00:20.920
And there is a lot of things that you can do, like maybe deleting data, printing a very structured

00:20.960 --> 00:23.920
like message or maybe doing some logging and so on.

00:23.920 --> 00:27.720
So you have a full control on what to do if there is an error.

00:27.720 --> 00:30.120
And of course we can do that in the stored procedure.

00:30.120 --> 00:31.240
So now let's check the quickly.

00:31.240 --> 00:33.800
The syntax it is usually has two parts.

00:33.840 --> 00:36.160
The first part is the try parts.

00:36.200 --> 00:37.440
So the syntax is like this.

00:37.440 --> 00:39.280
Begin try and try.

00:39.320 --> 00:42.040
So you are defining the boundaries of the try.

00:42.120 --> 00:45.880
And in between you can have all your SQL statements and your codes.

00:45.880 --> 00:48.280
And the second part is going to be the catch parts.

00:48.440 --> 00:51.200
So you say begin catch and end catch.

00:51.200 --> 00:52.760
So you are defining the boundaries.

00:52.760 --> 00:56.360
And then in between you can tell SQL what to do if there is like an error.

00:56.400 --> 00:59.720
So what is try and catch like the word it says try.

00:59.720 --> 01:02.800
It's like you are attempt to do something that might fail.

01:02.800 --> 01:09.560
So you are telling SQL Will try to execute this code so the SQL can go and try to execute your code.

01:09.560 --> 01:15.520
And if any error happens while executing your code, the SQL is going to jump to the second block and

01:15.520 --> 01:18.400
start doing whatever you have defined in the catch.

01:18.400 --> 01:22.760
But if there is no errors at all, this part will not be executed.

01:22.760 --> 01:24.920
So the catch is like your backup plan.

01:24.920 --> 01:28.840
If something goes wrong here, then go to the plan B and do something.

01:28.840 --> 01:30.840
So let's see the workflow of the try catch.

01:31.040 --> 01:34.200
So first the SQL can go and execute the try.

01:34.240 --> 01:35.320
And then it's going to check.

01:35.360 --> 01:36.400
Is there any error.

01:36.440 --> 01:40.000
If we don't have any error then everything ends and that's it.

01:40.000 --> 01:43.800
But while execution if the SQL fails any error what's going to happen.

01:43.800 --> 01:46.120
It's going to go and execute the catch.

01:46.160 --> 01:48.120
So as you can see the workflow is very simple.

01:48.120 --> 01:50.680
And this is what we mean with try and catch.

01:50.680 --> 01:52.880
So let's go back to SQL to have some example.

01:52.920 --> 01:53.240
All right.

01:53.240 --> 01:54.920
So now back to our stored procedure.

01:55.080 --> 01:57.920
Let's go and introduce an error inside our code.

01:57.920 --> 01:58.960
So let's go over here.

01:58.960 --> 02:05.040
And maybe in our query we're going to go and divide by zero which is of course a problem.

02:05.040 --> 02:06.880
So we have this error over here.

02:06.880 --> 02:11.640
And let's go and update the logic of our stored procedure.

02:11.680 --> 02:14.040
And now if you go and execute it.

02:14.040 --> 02:15.280
So let's go and do that.

02:15.320 --> 02:18.720
We will get an error saying yeah you cannot divide by zero.

02:18.720 --> 02:22.960
But now what I would like to do, I would like to have something else where we have customized message

02:22.960 --> 02:24.160
when error happens.

02:24.160 --> 02:28.400
So I would like to have the control on which information should be displayed if there is an issue.

02:28.400 --> 02:31.600
And in order to do that we have to use the try and catch.

02:31.600 --> 02:32.880
So it's going to be very simple.

02:33.000 --> 02:34.800
Now this is my whole code.

02:34.800 --> 02:39.520
So the whole thing from preparing to generate the report, the whole thing is my code.

02:39.520 --> 02:42.080
And we have to put the whole thing in a try.

02:42.120 --> 02:43.960
So how to do that exactly.

02:43.960 --> 02:47.640
After the first begin we're going to have another begin.

02:47.640 --> 02:48.720
But for the try.

02:48.760 --> 02:54.520
And now what we're going to do, we're going to go to the last end over here and have an end try.

02:54.560 --> 02:57.720
So with that, we put now the whole code inside the try.

02:57.760 --> 03:00.080
And after that we can introduce the catch.

03:00.280 --> 03:04.240
So begin catch and end catch.

03:04.280 --> 03:09.160
And now in between we have to tell SQL what can happen if we encounter any error.

03:09.160 --> 03:10.320
And here we can do many stuff.

03:10.320 --> 03:13.890
But I would like now to focus on customizing the error message.

03:13.890 --> 03:14.970
Let's start with the first one.

03:14.970 --> 03:16.410
So I'm going to say print.

03:17.210 --> 03:19.850
Let's say an error occurred.

03:21.130 --> 03:22.370
This is the first thing.

03:22.370 --> 03:25.930
Then on the next line I'm going to print more informations.

03:25.930 --> 03:27.610
And now we're going to say the error message.

03:27.610 --> 03:31.970
So error message double point space.

03:31.970 --> 03:37.850
And now we can go and use some predefined functions from SQL like for example the error message.

03:38.930 --> 03:43.010
This function can return the description of the error like the one we have here.

03:43.050 --> 03:50.450
Divide by zero error encountered and we can go and keep adding stuff the way that we need, like maybe

03:50.450 --> 03:54.170
the error number so we can have it like this.

03:54.210 --> 03:57.210
And for that we have as well a function called error number.

03:57.650 --> 04:00.570
And I think we have to cast this one because it is a number.

04:00.570 --> 04:03.970
And in the messages we have to have only a varchar.

04:04.010 --> 04:07.570
So this is going to be as an Varchar like this.

04:07.610 --> 04:10.770
And we can keep adding stuff to our message.

04:11.010 --> 04:13.210
Like for example let's take the error line.

04:13.490 --> 04:15.090
And for that we have as well a function.

04:15.090 --> 04:19.570
So it's going to be the error line like this.

04:19.570 --> 04:23.730
And we have to cast it because it is as well a number.

04:24.810 --> 04:30.090
And as well what is really important is the name of the stored procedure.

04:30.090 --> 04:32.450
So error procedure.

04:32.930 --> 04:38.210
And we have a function for that error procedure like this.

04:38.250 --> 04:39.130
It's going to be a string.

04:39.130 --> 04:40.850
So that's why I don't have to cast it.

04:40.850 --> 04:45.890
So now with that we have defined for SQL what to do if there is like an error in our code.

04:45.890 --> 04:48.210
So let's go and execute the whole thing.

04:49.090 --> 04:51.850
And now let's go and execute our stored procedure.

04:52.010 --> 04:53.330
So let's go and do that.

04:53.330 --> 04:56.450
So now as you can see in the output we are not getting any results.

04:56.450 --> 04:58.010
And it is not giving an error.

04:58.010 --> 05:01.370
But if you go to the messages you will see a very nice message.

05:01.370 --> 05:03.250
So it says an error is occurred.

05:03.250 --> 05:05.970
The error message is divided by zero.

05:05.970 --> 05:10.690
And we have the error number in which line and as well the stored procedure name.

05:10.690 --> 05:12.130
So as you can see it's amazing.

05:12.130 --> 05:17.810
This is how we use the try and catch in order to have more options on to control what can happen if

05:17.810 --> 05:18.690
there is an error.
